  "body": "Consider a module using non-latin characters like `你好.js`, with just the following source text:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconsole.log(import.meta.url);\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen importing this module into the browser, the output is `https://site.com/%E4%BD%A0%E5%A5%BD`.\r\n\r\nIt seems somewhat unnecessary to be letting the percent encoding behaviours of URL resolution affect these features on the web such that workflows using non-latin characters are more difficult than they need to be.\r\n\r\n`import.meta.url` is currently the only way in which these encodings are user visible. It could be possible to specify `import.meta.url` such that reverse encoding is applied to an appropriate subset of characters (eg not including control characters or path characters).\r\n\r\nWould there be interest in persuing such a direction here further? Feedback welcome.",
